<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mao Zedong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1893">1893</a>–<a href="/years/1976">1976</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Chairman Mao</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mao Zedong was a founding member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> who led the Chinese Soviet Republic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a> and emerged as party leader during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-march">Long March</a> of 1934–1935. After victory in the civil war he proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> in 1949 and ruled it until his death. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a> caused a famine that killed tens of millions, and he launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a> in 1966.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mao-zedong.jpg" alt="Mao Zedong" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chen Zhengqing (1917–1966) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mao_Zedong_1950_Portrait_(3x4_cropped)(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mao Zedong was born on 26 December 1893 near Shaoshan village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a>, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>. His father, Mao Yichang, was a formerly impoverished peasant who had become one of the wealthiest farmers in Shaoshan. He preferred classic novels such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romance-of-the-three-kingdoms">Romance of the Three Kingdoms</a> and Water Margin to the Confucian texts he was taught. At 13 he was married by arrangement to Luo Yixiu, whom he refused to recognise as his wife.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> he worked as assistant to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peking-university">Peking University</a> librarian Li Dazhao, an early Chinese Communist, whose articles on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> added Marxism to the doctrines of the Chinese revolutionary movement. Mao joined Li's Study Group and developed rapidly toward Marxism in the winter of 1919. In 1921 he became a founding member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> (CCP).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a> turned on the Communists in 1927, the CCP lost about 15,000 of its 25,000 members. The party founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Workers' and Peasants' Red Army</a>, and Mao was made its commander-in-chief. His Autumn Harvest Uprising against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changsha">Changsha</a> failed, and on 15 September he marched east with 1,000 survivors to the Jinggang Mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a>. In 1935 he became leader of the CCP during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-march">Long March</a>, a retreat to the Yan'an Soviet in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>. The civil war resumed after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s surrender in 1945, and the Nationalist government withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> in 1949.</p><p>Mao proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiananmen">Tiananmen</a> on 1 October 1949. He sent the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-volunteer-army">People's Volunteer Army</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> in October 1950. From 1958 to 1962 he oversaw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a>, which aimed to collectivise agriculture and industrialise the country and was followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-chinese-famine">Great Chinese Famine</a>. In 1966 he launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a>, marked by violent class struggle, destruction of historical artifacts and a cult of personality.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Mao died shortly after midnight on 9 September 1976, aged 82, after three heart attacks that year. Despite his request to be cremated, his embalmed body was later placed on permanent display in a mausoleum. Historians and others judge his rule in different ways. His policies are held to have caused the deaths of tens of millions of people, mainly through starvation, but also through persecution, prison labour and executions. He is also credited with ending decades of civil war, improving literacy, education and the status of women. On 27 June 1981 the CCP Central Committee adopted a resolution stating that his contributions to the revolution far outweighed his mistakes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mao Zedong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Kuomintang collapsed in 1927, the party built rural base areas, proclaimed the Chinese Soviet Republic in Jiangxi in 1931 and, driven out by Nationalist encirclement campaigns, made the Long March of 1934–35 to northern Shaanxi, where Yan’an became its base, during which <b>Mao Zedong</b> rose to the leadership.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 1 October 1949 the 10th Panchen Lama sent a telegram to Beijing welcoming the coming liberation of Tibet, and the government of <b>Mao Zedong</b> soon moved to reassert Chinese presence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet (1912-1951) · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1939 <b>Mao Zedong</b> called it a new stage in China’s bourgeois-democratic revolution against imperialism and feudalism, a step beyond the Revolution of 1911, and the historian Wang Gungwu notes that it came to be seen as the predecessor of the Cultural Revolution.</blockquote><a href="/movement/may-fourth-movement">May Fourth Movement · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Taiping mix of Christianity and radical social equality influenced Sun Yat-sen, and Chinese Communist historians, following <b>Mao Zedong</b>, called the rising proto-communist; John King Fairbank compared the Taiping with Mao’s communists.</blockquote><a href="/movement/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>After Nazi Germany’s defeat in 1945 the Soviet Union imposed communist dictatorships across Central and Eastern Europe, and in 1949 <b>Mao Zedong</b> proclaimed the People’s Republic of China.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Trade unions, youth leagues and women’s organisations serve as “transmission belts” between party and people, and the armed forces answer to the party; in <b>Mao Zedong</b>’s words, “the party commands the gun”.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · How It Works</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Shaoshan, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Zhongnanhai, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=27.91,112.48&amp;year=1946" aria-label="Shaoshan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7499 1141 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mao Zedong"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7949 1411h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7949 1411h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=27.91,112.48&amp;year=1946">What was here in 1946? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
